---

!! 1. Source Syntheses

!!! 1.1 Ink & Switch — Local-First Software (2019)

''Core Golden Principles:''

# ''No Spinners'': The application must work without waiting for the network. Local state holds primacy; remote state stays secondary.
# ''Your Work Is Not Trapped on One Device'': Data must move freely across all devices belonging to a user.
# ''The Network Is Optional'': A crash or server outage must never result in data loss or inability to work.
# ''Collaboration with Your Colleagues'': Multiple users can work on the same document simultaneously, including while offline, with automatic merge on reconnect.
# ''The Long Now'': Software must remain usable after the vendor disappears. Proprietary cloud lock-in poses an existential risk to data.
# ''Security and Privacy by Default'': The local-first model preserves privacy by construction: data lives on your machine first.
# ''You Retain Ultimate Ownership and Control'': The user's device, not a server, anchors the authoritative source of truth.

''Design Decisions Mapping to DreamNet:''

| Ink & Switch Principle | DreamNet mapping |
|---|---|
| Local copy holds primacy | device-vessel holds authoritative local CRDT repo; outer layers cannot override |
| Sync without coordinator | Automerge CRDT merge, no lock server |
| Software longevity | CID genesis artifact as immutable protocol root — data stays readable after forks |
| User ownership | Operator keypair on device-vessel; no outer layer holds signing keys |

''Specific Prior Art Patterns:''
* Triplicate — Ink & Switch's own local-first CMS prototype (demonstrates TW5-like wiki pattern)
* Automerge (developed at Ink & Switch) — the CRDT library DreamNet uses directly
* Pushpin — real-time collaborative tool with no server required

---

!!! 1.2 Automerge

''Core Golden Principles:''

# ''Network-agnostic data structure'': Automerge provides a pure data library; it does not know or care about transport.
# ''Immutable snapshots'': Every Automerge state object keeps immutable value semantics. Mutations produce new state objects.
# ''Automatic merge, no coordinator'': CRDT semantics guarantee convergence without a master node.
# ''Portable'': JS (Node, Electron, browser), Rust (WASM, C FFI, iOS). Same algorithm, same merge semantics, everywhere.
# ''Operation history'': Full change log. Branch, diff, merge workflows become possible as a first-class feature.

''Design Decisions:''
* ''Causal ordering via vector clocks'' (Lamport counters embedded in actor IDs): No wall clock trust required.
* ''Conflict representation, not resolution'': Automerge retains both concurrent values as a conflict object. Application chooses which to display; the CRDT never silently drops writes.
* ''Actor IDs'': Each Automerge peer carries a unique actor ID. This forms the primitive from which DreamNet's operator keypairs can be derived.
* ''automerge-repo'': Higher-level layer providing storage adapters (IndexedDB, filesystem) and network adapters (BroadcastChannel, WebSocket, WebRTC).

''Contrast with DreamNet:''
* Automerge lacks built-in access control. It merges everything from any writer with document access. DreamNet adds the capability layer (ABILITY_LADDER) //on top of// CRDT merge — a principled separation that Automerge explicitly endorses.

''Named Patterns:''
* RGA (Replicated Growable Array) — underlying sequence CRDT for text
* Columnar encoding (Kleppmann 2020) — reduces document overhead to ~1.5x size vs raw content
* automerge-repo StorageAdapter / NetworkAdapter interfaces — the extension points DreamNet device-vessels should implement

---

!!! 1.3 UCAN — User Controlled Authorization Networks

''Core Golden Principles:''

# ''Trustless delegation'': Authority can move from peer to peer without an online Authorization Server. The chain of cryptographic signatures provides the proof.
# ''Certificate capabilities, not ACLs'': Authorization uses a bearer token (like a movie ticket), not a list lookup (like a bouncer's clipboard). No server needs consultation at auth time.
# ''Principle of Least Authority (PoLA)'': Every delegation MUST either restate or attenuate (narrow) its authority. You cannot grant what you do not hold. You can always grant less.
# ''Inversion of control'': The resource owner issues UCANs to agents; no Authorization Server sits between requestor and resource. The resource anchors the root of trust.
# ''Content-addressed tokens'': Each UCAN hashes to a unique CIDv1. The token carries its own identity. Revocation proceeds by CID.
# ''Short expiry windows'': Keeping validity windows short limits blast radius.
# ''DID-based principals'': All actors use `did:key` (or equivalent), not email or username.

''Design Decisions:''
* ''Delegation chain as provenance log'': The UCAN chain provides an inherent audit trail. Every grant stays traceable to its root.
* ''Attenuation flows additive downward'': Capabilities can only narrow going down the chain.
* ''UCAN + CRDT'': In a pull model, an agent can hold a local CRDT replica and a UCAN invocation log, and gossiping both gives transparent provenance of authority in eventually consistent systems. ''This directly matches DreamNet's architecture.''
* ''Revocation'': Irreversible but possible. Once a CID gets revoked, all downstream delegations in the chain must fail. This directly grounds Lares' `revoke` tier.

''Contrast with DreamNet:''
* UCAN does not define group membership or shared contexts. Keyhive fills this gap. UCAN provides the per-token primitive; Keyhive provides the group management layer.
* UCAN's confinement problem (you cannot know all sub-delegations) surfaces a real risk in DreamNet's `move` / `admin` tiers.

''Named Patterns:''
* SPKI/SDSI — the 1990s predecessor; UCAN advances SPKI into web-native form
* ZCAP-LD (W3C CCG) — closely related W3C approach using JSON-LD
* Macaroon — MAC-based capability system; faster but weaker (rooted in server, not user)
* Biscuit — Datalog-based capabilities; allows richer policy expression
* CACAO — cross-chain capability; similar delegation model

---

!!! 1.4 Keyhive (Fission)

''Core Golden Principles (reconstructed from secondary references):''

# ''Group membership as CRDT'': Rather than a central server managing membership, group state replicates as a CRDT across all members.
# ''Device admission via out-of-band ceremony'': Adding a new device to a PersonaGroup does not require any server. A signed message from an existing device provides sufficient proof. This follows the "Seitan token exchange" pattern.
# ''Sentinel documents as group anchors'': Each group level (person, cabal, nexus) carries a sentinel document — a CID-addressed genesis artifact that defines the group's charter.
# ''Capability delegation through the group hierarchy'': A Keyhive PersonaGroup grants capabilities that can be delegated to devices. A MeshCabal grants capabilities to PersonaGroups.

''Design Decisions:''
* ''Local-first membership'': Group state replicates via the same CRDT mechanism as application data. No separate membership server.
* ''Cap-admin owns infrastructure bags'': The Keyhive pattern grounds DreamNet's cabalGroup ownership of "local infrastructure bags" with `cap=admin`.

''Named Patterns:''
* Local-First Auth (Herb Caudill) — non-certificate-based; uses CRDT to build group membership list. Very close to Keyhive's approach.
* Seitan token exchange — Keybase's device-admission ceremony; readable, out-of-band, no server needed.

---

!!! 1.5 Braid / HTTP State Synchronization

''Core Golden Principles:''

# ''State synchronization as a first-class HTTP primitive'': Braid extends HTTP to be a state //synchronization// protocol, with versioning, subscriptions, patches, and merge types.
# ''Interoperability across merge algorithms'': Different CRDTs and OT implementations speak the same Braid message format. No single algorithm gets mandated.
# ''P2P semantics levels'': Level 0 = today's HTTP. Level 1 = subscriptions. Level 2 = patches/versions/merges. Levels 3–4 = P2P semantics.

''Design Decisions:''
* ''Merge-type negotiation'': Client and server declare which CRDT/OT algorithm they support. Interoperability requires shared merge-type.
* ''History-pruning CRDTs'': Antimatter (from Braid group) marks the first CRDT that can garbage-collect history without losing merge ability.

''Contrast with DreamNet:''
* Braid-HTTP stays a candidate for the nexusGroup ↔ nexusGroup layer. DreamNet's `shared grammar` concept aligns with Braid's merge-type negotiation.

''Named Patterns:''
* Antimatter — history-pruning CRDT, solves unbounded growth
* Diamond Types (josephg) — fastest known text CRDT, O(log n) operations
* Sync9 / SyncX — JSON+text CRDT with OT compatibility bridge

---

!!! 1.6 Matrix Protocol

''Core Golden Principles:''

# ''No single point of control'': Room state replicates across all homeservers whose users participate.
# ''Event graphs (DAGs), not ordered logs'': Room history follows a DAG of events with causal ordering.
# ''Eventual consistency, not strong consistency'': Matrix explicitly optimizes for AP (Availability + Partition tolerance).
# ''Homeserver as sovereign unit'': Each homeserver manages its own users' accounts.
# ''State events vs. message events'': Persistent room state gets modeled separately from transient messages.
# ''Power levels, not capabilities'': Matrix uses a numeric power level (0–100) to gate actions.

''Design Decisions:''
* ''State resolution algorithm enforces deterministic, server-independent output'': Given the same event graph, any server must compute the same resolved state. Key to federation without coordination.
* ''Olm/Megolm E2E encryption'': Per-device keys; forward secrecy via double ratchet. Sessions run per-device (analogous to DreamNet device-vessels).

''Contrast with DreamNet:''
* Matrix lacks local-first mode. Homeservers run as always-on servers. DreamNet device-vessels can run as browser tabs — a fundamental architectural difference.
* Matrix's power level system appears coarser than DreamNet's ABILITY_LADDER but simpler.
* Matrix federation follows a symmetric pattern (server ↔ server). DreamNet's nexusGroup layer follows an explicitly asymmetric sovereignty model.

''Named Patterns:''
* Room Version state resolution algorithm — deterministic, commutative state merge across federated servers
* Olm (Double Ratchet) / Megolm (group ratchet) — per-device encryption session management
* MXC URI scheme — Matrix's content-addressed URI for attachments (analogous to `lar:///`)

''Deepening — authenticated media + the authority-pinning trap:''
* ''`mxc://` authenticated media (MSC3916)'': Matrix retrofitted access control onto media — a client MUST present an auth token to a `/_matrix/client/v1/media` endpoint before the origin homeserver serves the bytes. The retrofit itself testifies to the original sin: `mxc://{server-name}/{media-id}` ''pins content to one authority'' (the naming homeserver) behind an ''opaque, non-hash media-id''. The identifier names //where a server keeps a thing//, never //what the thing is//. Two failure modes follow by construction: the origin homeserver going dark orphans the media, and the opaque id lets no holder verify what it fetched. Matrix's leaked-URL breach (the sin the resolution design cites) traces to the same root — a fetchable URL fused resolution, authorization, and read into one string.
* ''The DreamNet fix — content-address drops the pin'': `cid = BLAKE3(ciphertext)` names //what// the bytes carry, so any holder verifies blind and the naming authority drops out of the identity; a stable logical handle + provider-locality hint replaces `{server-name}` (indirection map, WNFS private-forest), so no origin homeserver holds the media hostage. The three-gate `resolveByCid` chain (may-talk · may-resolve-this-cid · may-read) keeps split what `mxc://` + the leaked-URL fused. <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/content-resolution">>

---

!!! 1.7 Gordon Brander / Subconscious / Noosphere

''Core Golden Principles (reconstructed from secondary references):''

# ''The Small Web'': Decentralization fails if users must run servers. Sovereignty requires that the user's device IS the server.
# ''Noosphere'': Protocol for a user-sovereign note graph. Content-addressed (CID), user-keyed (DID), linked (URI).
# ''Permissionless composition'': Any app can link to any note by its CID without requesting permission.
# ''Shared language, not shared data'': Interoperability across networks comes from agreeing on vocabulary (ontology), not from centralizing data.
# ''The Sphere'': User's unit of sovereignty — a CID-rooted DAG of notes owned by a keypair.

''Named Patterns:''
* Noosphere protocol — content-addressed, DID-keyed note graph
* Sphere — user-sovereign data container with CID root

---

!!! 1.8 Spritely Institute / OCapN

''Core Golden Principles:''

# ''Object capabilities support distributed programming'': In OCapN/Goblins, sending a message to a networked object looks identical to calling a local method.
# ''Capabilities ARE references'': In a pure ocap system, having a reference to an object IS having permission to use it.
# ''Promise pipelining'': Results of remote calls can be passed as arguments to the next call without waiting for the network round-trip.
# ''3rd-party handoffs'': A peer can introduce two other peers to each other by passing a capability reference.
# ''CRDTs + capabilities compose cleanly'' (Nov 2025 Spritely blog): "Composing capability security and conflict-free replicated data types" — direct prior art for DreamNet's combination of Automerge + ABILITY_LADDER.

''Design Decisions:''
* ''Netlayers as transport abstraction'': OCapN runs over Tor, libp2p, TCP+TLS, IBC. Transport-independent.
* ''Confinement via reference discipline'': Capabilities cannot be forged.

''Contrast with DreamNet:''
* OCapN provides live object capabilities (call semantics). DreamNet uses certificate capabilities (UCAN-style bearer tokens). Different points in the capability design space:
** OCapN: fine-grained, live, requires connectivity
** UCAN/DreamNet: coarse-grained, offline-verifiable, partition-tolerant

''Named Patterns:''
* CapTP (Capability Transport Protocol) — the heart of OCapN
* Petnames — human-readable mappings over cryptographic identifiers
* Magenc — content-encrypted store; capability-protected immutable blobs

---

!!! 1.9 IPFS Content Addressing

''Core Golden Principles:''

# ''Location independence'': CIDs name content, not servers.
# ''Cryptographic integrity'': Any difference in content produces a different CID.
# ''Self-describing'': CIDv1 embeds the hash algorithm, codec, and encoding in the identifier itself.
# ''DAG structure'': Large content gets chunked into blocks linked by CIDs, forming a Merkle DAG.
# ''CID profiles for reproducibility'': CID profiles document canonical parameter sets for interoperability.

''Design Decisions:''
* ''`dag-cbor` as canonical encoding'': UCAN mandates DAG-CBOR for signing; IPLD's DAG-CBOR provides the natural bridge.
* ''Multiformats'': Self-describing binary formats that can evolve without breaking existing CIDs.

''Note'': DreamNet uses CIDs as stable identifiers but may retrieve content over any transport. Does not require IPFS infrastructure.

''Named Patterns:''
* IPLD (InterPlanetary Linked Data) — universal data model for content-addressed DAGs
* Merkle DAG — the underlying data structure
* Multibase / Multihash / Multicodec — self-describing primitives enabling codec-agnostic CIDs

---

!!! 1.10 CRDTs in Production

''Core Golden Principles:''

# ''Logical clocks over system time'': System clocks cannot be trusted. Lamport counters and Hybrid Logical Clocks (HLCs) provide causally correct ordering.
# ''Two-timestamp pattern'': For efficient delta sync, retain both `row_time` (merge semantics) and `local_row_time` (peer-seen state). Conflation causes proxy divergence.
# ''Deterministic tie-breaking'': Concurrent edits at the same logical time must be resolved by a deterministic, total-order comparator.
# ''CRDTs work in centralized systems too'': Git provides the canonical example.
# ''Performance no longer blocks adoption'': Modern CRDTs handle 100KB documents efficiently. 6 million text edits/second in Rust (Diamond Types).

''Named Patterns:''
* HLC (Hybrid Logical Clock) — combines wall clock with Lamport counter for causal correctness
* RGA / LSEQ / YATA — sequence CRDT algorithms for ordered text
* Delta-CRDTs — send only the delta (diff) between states; critical for efficient sync

---

!!! 1.11 Capability-Based Security (E Language / Mark Miller / OCapN)

''Core Golden Principles:''

# ''Capabilities as the only access mechanism'': In a pure ocap system, the only way to affect an object comes through a reference (capability). No ambient authority.
# ''No separation of authority from reference'': Possession of the capability carries authorization. This eliminates confused deputy attacks by construction.
# ''Composability'': Capability systems compose without interference.
# ''Attenuation by wrapping'': Any capability can be wrapped in a facet that attenuates its powers.
# ''Principle of Least Authority (POLA)'': Grant only the minimum capability needed for a task.
# ''The Structure of Authority (Miller et al.)'': Treating security as a separate concern failed repeatedly. Security requires integration with knowledge of what constitutes least authority.

''Named Patterns:''
* E language (Mark Miller) — the original object capability programming language
* Vat model — each process runs as a "vat"; objects communicate via capabilities over async message passing; precursor to DreamNet's vessel model
* SPKI/SDSI — Simple Public Key Infrastructure; DreamNet's keypair model aligns with SPKI
* Confused deputy problem — capability systems eliminate this by making authority explicit in references

---

!!! 1.12 Hypercore Protocol (Holepunch/Pears)

''Core Golden Principles:''

# ''One writer, many readers'': A Hypercore provides an append-only log with a single writer (keypair) and unlimited readers.
# ''Reading IS authorization'': Possessing the Hypercore `key` (public key) authorizes reading.
# ''Sparse replication'': Peers can replicate only the blocks they need.
# ''Autobase = multi-writer CRDT'': Multi-writer layer that merges contributions from multiple per-user Hypercores.
# ''Hyperswarm = topic-based peer discovery'': No central server required.

''Design Decisions:''
* ''Single-writer append-only log'': Each vessel writes to its own log; Autobase handles multi-writer merge. Maps cleanly to DreamNet's device-vessel.
* ''Discovery key ≠ read key'': Separation of discovery (Hyperswarm topic) from read authorization (`core.key`) — a security design decision DreamNet should mirror.
* ''Corestore'': Manages multiple Hypercores under a single namespace.

''Contrast with DreamNet:''
* DreamNet uses Automerge (any-writer CRDT) rather than Hypercore (single-writer append-only log).
* Hypercore's single-writer model makes it easier to reason about who wrote what — important for ABILITY_LADDER enforcement.

''Named Patterns:''
* Hypercore — secure, distributed append-only log
* Autobase — multi-writer view over many Hypercores
* Hyperswarm + HyperDHT — topic-based P2P discovery without a tracker server
* Hyperbee — append-only B-tree over a Hypercore
* Hyperdrive — full filesystem over Hypercore

---

!!! 1.13 Iroh / n0 — Verified Blob Streaming

''Core Golden Principles:''

# ''Content-address on BLAKE3, verify while you stream'': A blob's identity comes from `BLAKE3(bytes)`. Because BLAKE3 hashes a Merkle tree of 1KiB chunks, a fetcher verifies each incoming slice against the root hash //as it arrives//, never after the whole payload lands.
# ''Range-verify small slices from an untrusted peer'': The `bao` verified-streaming format ships each ≤16KiB range beside its Merkle proof. A holder who never trusts its source still accepts or rejects each slice on its own proof — trust rides the hash, not the peer.
# ''Blob / document split'': Documents store the //hash//, never the payload. Mutable structure stays small and syncs cheaply; the heavy bytes live in the content-addressed blob store, fetched lazily by hash.
# ''The reference carries its own discovery'': A `BlobTicket` bundles `hash + NodeAddr` — the //what// and a //where-to-start// travel together, so resolution needs no separate lookup step for the first hop.
# ''Collections without a container format'': A `HashSeq` (a blob whose bytes are a sequence of child hashes) names a set of blobs as one addressable thing — a Merkle-DAG collection built from the same primitive.

''Design Decisions:''
* ''Outboard proof'': The BLAKE3 outboard (the tree nodes minus the data) rides beside the blob, so range-verification costs proof-bytes, not a re-hash of the whole body.
* ''~16KiB inline threshold'': Bytes below the range-verify granularity ride inline; larger bodies land on the filesystem and stream range-verified.

''Contrast with DreamNet:''
* Iroh addresses //plaintext//; DreamNet addresses //ciphertext// (`cid = BLAKE3(ciphertext)`), so an untrusted holder verifies and repairs blind without a confirmation oracle. The content-addressed tier `@cad` (bytes in the `cid/<hash>` KIND-plane, handles riding the publicity-tiered bags) lifts Iroh's blob/doc split (skinny TW5 handle stores the hash; bytes leave the CRDT), the ~16KiB inline/range-verify boundary, and the BlobTicket `hash + NodeAddr` hint directly. <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/content-resolution">>

''Named Patterns:''
* bao — BLAKE3 verified-streaming format (range proofs)
* BlobTicket — `hash + NodeAddr`, discovery collapsed into the reference
* HashSeq — hash-sequence blob as a Merkle-DAG collection
* Tags (pin) vs. untagged (GC-eligible) — mark-and-sweep retention from tags

---

!!! 1.14 WNFS (Fission) — Content-Address ∧ Capability, Fused

''Core Golden Principles:''

# ''The identifier answers what/where; the token answers who-may'': A CID says //what the bytes are and where to find them//; a UCAN says //who may act on them//. WNFS holds both as one coherent surface — content-addressing and capability fused, never one substituting for the other.
# ''Private-forest indirection'': A private WNFS node lives behind a HAMT (hash-array-mapped trie) keyed by a stable name; the name maps to the //current// encrypted CID. The name survives while the CID underneath re-keys — a stable logical handle over shifting ciphertext.
# ''Encrypted-at-rest, structure-preserving'': The forest structure replicates and syncs while every node stays encrypted; a holder routes and stores without reading.

''Design Decisions:''
* ''HAMT as the indirection map'': The stable-name → current-CID mapping is itself small mutable metadata, so it rides the mutable structure layer (the Automerge doc in DreamNet), not the blob store.

''Contrast with DreamNet:''
* DreamNet lifts WNFS's private-forest indirection directly: the skinny handle names a //stable logical name//, resolved through an indirection map (logical name → current cid) so a content re-key survives without breaking the handle. WNFS's what/where ∧ who-may fusion maps onto the three-gate split DreamNet keeps un-fused (address vs. cap vs. decrypt). <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/content-resolution">>

''Named Patterns:''
* Private Forest — encrypted namespace over content-addressed nodes
* HAMT indirection — stable-name → current-cid, re-key-survivable
* CID + UCAN fusion — what/where ∧ who-may as one surface

---

!!! 1.15 Tahoe-LAFS — Convergent Dedup ∧ Cap Separation

''Core Golden Principles:''

# ''Scoped convergence secret — dedup without a confirmation oracle'': The encryption key derives from `hash(convergence-secret + plaintext)`. Identical plaintext under the //same// secret converges to the same ciphertext (dedup); under a //different// secret it does not (no cross-tenant confirmation-of-file leak). Global convergent encryption leaks file-presence (the CFA / learn-remaining-information attack); Tahoe's //scoped// secret closes that leak while keeping dedup within a trust boundary.
# ''Verify-cap ⊥ read-cap'': A verify capability lets a holder check and repair a share //without// the read capability. An untrusted storage holder repairs corrupted ciphertext blind — it proves the bytes intact against the verify-cap, never decrypting them.
# ''Cap hierarchy'': write-cap ⊇ read-cap ⊇ verify-cap — each strictly attenuates the last, so a holder is handed exactly the authority its role needs.
# ''Provider-independent security'': Confidentiality and integrity hold even if every storage server is hostile; trust rides the caps, never the servers.

''Contrast with DreamNet:''
* DreamNet's `@cad` key = `hash(per-operator convergence-secret + plaintext)` lifts Tahoe's scoped-convergence secret verbatim — dedup within one operator's `@cad`, zero cross-operator leak, beating both global-convergent (leaks) and random-key (no dedup). And ciphertext-addressing (`cid = BLAKE3(ciphertext)`) enacts verify-cap ⊥ read-cap: an untrusted peer verifies/pins/repairs against the cid without the decryption key (node routes ciphertext, never decrypts — the MLS Delivery-Service keel). <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/content-resolution">>

''Named Patterns:''
* Convergence secret (scoped) — dedup without a confirmation oracle
* verify-cap ⊥ read-cap — blind repair by an untrusted holder
* write ⊇ read ⊇ verify — strictly attenuating cap hierarchy

---

!!! 1.16 Beelay (Automerge / Ink & Switch) — Skinny Refs ∧ Ciphertext Relay

''Core Golden Principles:''

# ''Skinny blob_ref — hash-if-big, inline-if-tiny'': A reference carries the //bytes// when they are small enough to be cheaper inline, and carries a //hash// when they are large. The threshold decides representation; the reference stays uniform to its reader either way.
# ''Sedimentree — layered, compactable history'': Change history settles into strata — recent fine-grained ops over compacted older layers — so the log stays bounded without losing merge ability (history-pruning applied to the sync layer itself).
# ''Ciphertext-only relay'': The sync server relays and stores //ciphertext// alone. It coordinates delivery and never holds a decryption key — the relay carries sealed bytes, sovereignty stays with the endpoints.

''Contrast with DreamNet:''
* Beelay's skinny `blob_ref` (hash-if-big / inline-if-tiny) restates the `@cad` inline/range boundary at the ''sync-reference'' grain; the sedimentree names the compaction strategy DreamNet's large-bodies-leave-the-CRDT move enacts (heavy bytes stop accreting in the op log). And ciphertext-only relay enacts the substrate-not-sovereignty keel — a node MAY carry sealed bytes, MUST NOT read them; the relay-floor axis (blind carriage) restated at the CRDT-sync layer. <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/content-resolution">>

''Named Patterns:''
* blob_ref (skinny) — hash-if-big / inline-if-tiny, uniform to the reader
* sedimentree — layered compactable history (bounded log, merge-preserving)
* ciphertext-only relay — coordinate delivery, never hold a key

---

!! 2. Layer Mapping: Prior Art → DreamNet

> ''Frame note (post-split residue).'' This 2026-05-28 mapping reads the prior art onto the then-current ''single five-layer stack''. The architecture later ''split that stack into two orthogonal axes'' — INFRA (FLOW · carriage, relay-floor → lararium → nexusGroup → DreamNet) ⊥ WHO (read · membership, vessel-key → personaGroup → cabalGroup), crossing at the vessel. The component rows below still hold one-for-one; only the //ladder// they sit in dissolved into a 2D coordinate. §4 #divergences carries the correction; the live model stands at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/dreamnet-architecture#/two-axis-topology">>.

!!! Layer 1: device-vessel

| Feature | Prior Art | Maturity |
|---|---|---|
| Local-first data store | Automerge (Ink & Switch) | Production |
| Sovereign keypair | UCAN `did:key`, Hypercore writer keypair | Production |
| Browser + server runtime | Automerge-repo (BroadcastChannel, WebSocket) | Production |
| TW5 wiki as local app | TiddlyWiki self-contained single-file model | Decades of production |
| Actor ID per vessel | Automerge actor IDs, E language Vat model | Research + production |
| Offline-first writes | All local-first sources | Well-established principle |

''Assessment'': Device-vessel stands as the strongest-grounded layer. All required components show production implementations plus strong theoretical backing.

---

!!! Layer 2: personGroup

| Feature | Prior Art | Maturity |
|---|---|---|
| Group as CRDT membership doc | Local-First Auth (Caudill), Keyhive | Research + early production |
| Out-of-band device admission ceremony | Seitan token exchange (Keybase), UCAN delegation | Production |
| Multi-device identity | Matrix (per-device keys), Olm/Megolm | Production |
| Sentinel genesis document | UCAN root CID, Hypercore writer key | Pattern well-known |
| Capability delegation per device | UCAN attenuation chain | Production spec |

''Assessment'': PersonaGroup draws strong support from prior art. The Seitan/Local-First Auth pattern for ceremony-based device admission offers the closest analogue. Primary risk: no single system combines all three (CRDT membership + ceremony + UCAN delegation) in production.

---

!!! Layer 3: cabalGroup

| Feature | Prior Art | Maturity |
|---|---|---|
| Group-of-groups with charter | Matrix rooms, Keyhive MeshCabal | Research |
| Charter-as-genesis-CID | IPFS CID semantics, UCAN root | Pattern well-known |
| Shared ontology enforcement | No direct prior art; closest = Matrix event type namespacing | Partial |
| Infrastructure bag ownership (cap=admin) | UCAN `admin` delegation | Pattern well-known |
| Intra-cabal CRDT sync | Automerge-repo multi-peer | Production |

''Assessment'': cabalGroup carries clear design intent but fewer direct precedents as a //combined// construct. Individual pieces exist in prior art but no source assembles them in the exact DreamNet configuration.

---

!!! Layer 4: nexusGroup

| Feature | Prior Art | Maturity |
|---|---|---|
| Federation without central authority | Matrix federation, SMTP | Production (Matrix) |
| Multiple cabalGroups cooperating | ActivityPub federation | Production |
| No single point of control | Matrix (rooms span servers), Hyperswarm topics | Production |
| Interop across implementations | Braid merge-type negotiation | Active development |
| Federated identity | DIDs, Matrix @user:server syntax | Production |

''Assessment'': nexusGroup maps closely to Matrix's federation model — the most mature prior art. Key divergence: Matrix homeservers run always-on and assume server-to-server connectivity. DreamNet's nexusGroup must tolerate cabalGroups going offline indefinitely. Braid's merge-type negotiation offers the best candidate for cross-cabal interoperability protocol.

---

!!! Layer 5: DreamNet

| Feature | Prior Art | Maturity |
|---|---|---|
| Shared grammar as interop surface | Braid (merge-types), ActivityPub (vocabulary), IPLD | Production (individually) |
| URI scheme as grammar root | Matrix MXC, IPFS ipfs://, lar:/// | Pattern well-known |
| Legibility across partitions and protocol forks | No direct prior art for all-5-layers | Novel |
| No outer layer commands inner | E language vat authority model | Research |
| SharktoothSigil / mana vocabulary | No direct prior art | Novel |

''Assessment'': The outermost DreamNet layer stands as the most novel. No existing system defines a cross-federation shared grammar that survives protocol forks and network partitions as a //first-class design goal//. ActivityPub comes closest but lacks the sovereignty properties and CRDT-backed state.

---

!! 3. Where DreamNet Aligns with Best Practices

!!! Strong Alignments

''1. Local-first data primacy''
DreamNet's "every write originates in a sovereign vessel" expresses a direct instantiation of the Ink & Switch local-first ideal. All major local-first systems agree on this.

''2. CRDT for conflict-free merge''
Using Automerge specifically marks a pragmatic choice aligned with the field's best option as of 2025-2026. Automerge's network-agnosticism and actor-ID-based causal ordering provide correct primitives.

''3. Content-addressed genesis artifact (CID)''
Using a CID as the protocol root marks a well-established pattern in UCAN, IPFS, Noosphere, and Hypercore. This provides tamper-evident identity, location independence, and an immutable reference for protocol versioning.

''4. Capability-based security over ACLs''
The ABILITY_LADDER defines a principled capability system. This aligns with the deepest prior art (Mark Miller, E language, UCAN, OCapN). The UCAN spec's own motivation section could describe DreamNet's rationale verbatim.

''5. Keypair-per-vessel sovereign identity''
Each device-vessel carrying its own keypair matches the UCAN/Hypercore/Matrix model. Private keys do not leave the vessel. Authority moves through delegation, not key sharing.

''6. No outer layer can substitute inner intent''
The capability principle applied to the layer structure: you cannot grant more than you hold. In formal terms, DreamNet's sovereignty hierarchy holds monotone order.

''7. Out-of-band device admission ceremony''
QR/file/DM ceremony maps exactly to the Seitan token exchange pattern (Keybase, cited in UCAN prior art).

---

!! 4. Where DreamNet Diverges and Why

!!! Principled Divergences

''1. Browser-tab as a sovereign vessel''
Most prior art (Matrix, Hypercore) assumes always-on servers as the fundamental unit. DreamNet's browser tab as first-class sovereign vessel appears more aggressive than any production system. The risks (ephemeral IndexedDB, no stable network address, short lifetime) sit at engineering level, not architectural level.

''2. Two-axis topology vs. flat federation''
Matrix and ActivityPub use two layers: client and server/homeserver. DreamNet runs two orthogonal axes crossing at the vessel — INFRA (relay-floor → lararium → nexusGroup → DreamNet) carrying FLOW, SOCIAL (vessel-key → personaGroup → cabalGroup) carrying WHO — each with explicit sovereignty semantics at its boundaries. The principled motivation: carriage (who relays whose sealed bytes) stays orthogonal to membership (who may read), so a node carries without reading (the relay floor); the shrine tiers (household → neighborhood → civic) still map to real social scale. Risk: coordination complexity where the axes cross. (See <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/dreamnet-architecture#/two-axis-topology">>.)

''3. Shared grammar over shared implementation''
DreamNet explicitly rejects shared implementation as the interoperability surface. ActivityPub uses both. DreamNet's approach appears more philosophically consistent but harder to bootstrap: new implementations must correctly interpret shared vocabulary without reference to a canonical implementation. This requires excellent SharktoothSigil specification and strong test fixtures.

''4. TiddlyWiki as the embedded data layer''
No other distributed system uses TW5 as a knowledge store. Strength: TW5's mature ecosystem, self-contained model, plugin architecture. Risk: TW5 lacks native design for CRDT merge. Closest analogue: Triplicate project (Ink & Switch).

''5. mana/manao/manaoio trust-weight lattice''
The three-tier trust weight system lacks direct prior art in the literature reviewed. Novel ontological primitives require careful formal specification to avoid semantic drift across implementations.

!!! Potentially Risky Divergences

''1. No explicit revocation mechanism at the CRDT layer''
UCAN explicitly specifies revocation (by CID). DreamNet's `revoke` tier implies intent but the mechanism for propagating revocation in an eventually-consistent CRDT system lacks explicit specification. In AP systems, revocation ranks as the hardest problem.

''2. Shared grammar survivability across protocol forks''
The claim that DreamNet "legibly spans network partitions and protocol forks" appears ambitious. ActivityPub encountered sustained difficulty with this even with a reference implementation.

''3. Authority confinement in the presence of sub-delegation''
The UCAN spec explicitly flags the confinement problem: certificate capabilities cannot guarantee knowledge of all sub-delegations. The architecture should specify a depth limit or attenuation requirement on outward delegation.

---

!! 5. Identified Gaps

!!! Gap 1: Explicit Revocation Propagation Protocol

''Source'': UCAN spec §Lifecycle, Hypercore, Matrix

''Description'': DreamNet carries a `revoke` tier but no specified mechanism for propagating revocations to all vessels that might cache capabilities. The architecture should specify:
* How revocations get represented in the CRDT (as CRDT operations, so they merge)
* What maximum acceptable propagation delay the protocol permits
* How a vessel should behave when it holds a capability without verified un-revoked status

''Prior art'': UCAN Revocation sub-spec; Keyhive revocation via group-CRDT; Local-First Auth revocation event

''Now addressed:'' The content-resolution design splits revocation into two forward-only modes. ''authz-revoke'' drops the `#has` cap + drops the retention tag (cheap, forward-immediate) and rides a grow-only revocation-CID set that merges as a CRDT operation (Gate 2). ''content-revoke'' re-keys → new ciphertext → new cid → indirection-map update. Already-fetched bytes stay UNRECALLABLE by construction — no-global-now forbids deletion-across-caches, so the design names that boundary rather than pretending past it. <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/content-resolution">>

---

!!! Gap 2: Document Growth / CRDT Compaction Strategy

''Source'': josephg article on CRDT futures, Braid Antimatter, vlcn.io

''Description'': Automerge's op log grows unboundedly with edits. A long-lived device-vessel with years of edit history will accumulate significant storage overhead. No provision for:
* Snapshotting / compacting the Automerge log
* Garbage-collecting tombstoned (deleted) content
* Differentiating "protocol history that must be retained" from "edit history that can be compacted"

''Prior art'': Antimatter (Braid group) — first history-pruning text CRDT; Automerge's own compaction research; Git's `gc` and pack files

''Now addressed:'' The content-resolution design attacks the dominant growth term directly — ''large bodies LEAVE the CRDT''. A carrier body becomes a skinny TW5 handle (hash + hint); only small mutable metadata rides the Automerge doc, while the heavy bytes land in the `@cad` content-addressed store (Iroh/Beelay blob-doc split). The op log stops accreting media; Beelay's sedimentree names the compaction shape for the residual sync history. (Edit-history tombstone GC — Gap 2's other leg — stays open.) <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/content-resolution">>

---

!!! Gap 3: Petnames / Human-Readable Layer Over Cryptographic IDs

''Source'': Spritely Institute "Petnames" paper, Subconscious Noosphere

''Description'': DreamNet principals use keypairs (did:key or equivalent) plus CIDs. These identifiers provide unforgeability but not human readability. No named mechanism gives users human-readable names for other vessels, cabalGroups, or genesis artifacts. The SharktoothSigil vocabulary may fill part of this gap but lacks description as a petname system.

''Prior art'': Petnames paper (Spritely); Noosphere sphere naming; Matrix display names / room aliases; Keybase username-to-key attestation

---

!!! Gap 4: Synchronization Protocol Specification for nexusGroup Layer

''Source'': Braid, Matrix Server-Server API

''Description'': DreamNet specifies CRDT semantics for intra-vessel and intra-cabal sync, but not the wire protocol for nexusGroup ↔ nexusGroup communication. Needs:
* A specified transport envelope for inter-cabal sync (Braid-HTTP stays a candidate)
* A merge-type negotiation mechanism for cross-cabal interoperability
* An explicit anti-entropy protocol (how do nexusGroups discover what they're missing?)

''Prior art'': Braid-HTTP (IETF draft); Matrix Server-Server API; Hyperswarm (topic-based peer discovery); Set reconciliation

''Now addressed (discovery / anti-entropy leg):'' The content-resolution design specifies a ''DHT-free'' discovery chain for resolving content across the mesh: hint-named provider (Iroh BlobTicket `hash + NodeAddr`, discovery collapsed into the reference) → connected bag-peers `want-have` (Bitswap) → named bag-scoped tracker/rendezvous (opt-in, signed, replaceable) → declared-unresolved = PENDING (a first-class lawful state, never a thrown error). The one structurally-forced global Kademlia DHT stays BEHIND a causal-island boundary; the per-nexus `@oracle` root/index docId serves as a rendezvous ADDRESS, not a shared present. (The merge-type-negotiation envelope leg stays open.) <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/content-resolution">>

---

!!! Gap 5: Hybrid Logical Clocks (HLC) for Event Ordering

''Source'': vlcn.io CRDT guide, distributed systems literature

''Description'': The architecture does not specify event timestamping within a device-vessel. Using system time stays explicitly unsafe. Automerge uses Lamport counters internally, but:
* No specified mapping between Automerge's internal causal ordering and human-visible timestamps
* The mana/manao/manaoio lattice may need causal ordering to determine which trust-weight values count as "more recent"
* Cross-vessel event ordering for nexusGroup and DreamNet layers lacks specification

''Prior art'': Hybrid Logical Clocks (HLC) — combines wall clock for human readability with Lamport counter for causal correctness. Used in CockroachDB, vlcn.io's cr-sqlite.

---

!!! Gap 6: Discovery Key / Read Key Separation

''Source'': Hypercore protocol

''Description'': Hypercore explicitly separates the `discoveryKey` (find peers) from the `key` (read capability). DreamNet's architecture does not explicitly specify this separation. A device-vessel advertising its presence for peer discovery should not reveal its read capabilities.

''Prior art'': Hypercore `core.discoveryKey` vs. `core.key`; UCAN audience field; Magenc (Spritely) capability-protected content addressing

''Now addressed:'' The content-resolution design keeps the two keys strictly separate via verify-cap ⊥ read-cap (Tahoe). The address a peer advertises and verifies against reads `cid = BLAKE3(''ciphertext'')` — a discovery/verify surface that reveals nothing readable; the decryption key rides a separate CGKA/BeeKEM channel and never touches the node (node routes and verifies ciphertext, MUST NOT decrypt — the MLS Delivery-Service keel). Advertising presence for resolution exposes no read capability. <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/content-resolution">>

---

!!! Gap 7: Formal Specification of ABILITY_LADDER Attenuation Rules

''Source'': UCAN §Attenuation, E language capability theory

''Description'': The ABILITY_LADDER gets described, but the //attenuation rules// still lack formal specification:
* Can a `write` holder delegate `read`? (Yes, by attenuation — should be explicit)
* Can an `admin` delegate `revoke` to a vessel that only holds `write`? (Likely no — needs specification)
* What does attenuation look like at the nexusGroup layer where inner vessels may not be visible?

''Prior art'': UCAN §Attenuation + §Authority + §Command hierarchy; E language `Facet` pattern; OCapN attenuated forwarders

---

!!! Gap 8: Absent Network: Long-Partition Behavior

''Source'': Ink & Switch local-first essay, Braid AP-vs-CP analysis, Matrix CAP-AP tradeoff

''Description'': The architecture specifies eventual consistency but not behavior during extended partitions:
* If a device-vessel spends years offline, then returns online, how does its CRDT state merge without re-fetching all history?
* If a cabalGroup forks (two sub-groups lose contact and both continue operating), does a mechanism exist for later reconciliation?
* The DreamNet layer gets described as surviving "protocol forks" — but no fork reconciliation protocol appears.

''Prior art'': Matrix state resolution algorithm (deterministic fork merge); Braid fork semantics; Automerge's built-in merge-on-reconnect

---

!! Summary Table

| Category | Finding |
|---|---|
| Strongest alignment | Local-first primacy, Automerge CRDT, CID genesis artifact, UCAN-style capability ladder, keypair sovereignty |
| Principled divergences | Browser-tab vessel, two-axis topology (relay-floor, blind carriage), shared grammar over shared implementation |
| Potentially risky divergences | Revocation propagation, confinement in certificate cap model, mana lattice novelty |
| ''Gap 1'' | Explicit revocation propagation protocol — ''now addressed'' (two-mode: authz grow-only CID set + content re-key) |
| ''Gap 2'' | CRDT compaction / document growth strategy — ''now addressed'' (large bodies leave the CRDT into `@cad`; tombstone-GC leg open) |
| ''Gap 3'' | Petnames / human-readable layer over cryptographic IDs |
| ''Gap 4'' | nexusGroup wire protocol (Braid-HTTP stays candidate) — ''discovery/anti-entropy leg addressed'' (DHT-free hint→peers→bag-tracker) |
| ''Gap 5'' | Hybrid Logical Clocks for cross-vessel event ordering |
| ''Gap 6'' | Discovery key / read key separation — ''now addressed'' (verify-cap ⊥ read-cap; ciphertext-address vs. decryption key) |
| ''Gap 7'' | Formal attenuation rules for ABILITY_LADDER |
| ''Gap 8'' | Long-partition / fork reconciliation behavior |

//Gaps 1 · 2 · 4 · 6 land under the converged content-resolution design (large bodies leave the CRDT: skinny handle + `@cad` ciphertext-addressed CAS + `resolveByCid` over three gates + DHT-free discovery). See <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/content-resolution">>.//

